# Copy to <config>/project-dashboard/.env, where <config> is $SYSTEM_TOOLS_CONFIG
# (default ~/.config/system).
#
# Interpreter for `dashboard.sh tile` and `dashboard.sh queue-open`, the two
# subcommands a person runs from a shell. It is not the interpreter the
# dashboard's own pages use: the LaunchAgent starts the server under
# SD_DASHBOARD_PYTHON, and a Resources view runs the tile as a child of that
# server, under the server's interpreter. Two binaries, therefore, and macOS
# grants Full Disk Access to a binary, so each needs its own grant for the
# vault under ~/Documents.
#
# Set this to Homebrew python. Xcode's /usr/bin/python3 does not work: macOS
# ignores user FDA grants for it under launchd. After a brew upgrade, re-grant
# FDA to the new Cellar Python.app — until then a tile run from a shell fails
# every vault-reading tab, naming the interpreter to grant. `dashboard.sh
# grants` probes this path and SD_DASHBOARD_PYTHON's in one run and names the
# one that lacks the grant; from a Terminal its answers are the shell's own
# access, which it says and exits 3 for, so run it from launchd to measure the
# binaries. This file is sourced by dashboard.sh, so the value here wins over
# an exported DASHBOARD_PYTHON, and `grants` reports the path it would use.
DASHBOARD_PYTHON=/opt/homebrew/bin/python3

# The Obsidian vault collectors.py reads, and the checkout root it scans. Both
# are optional; the defaults are ~/Documents/Vault and ~/repos. Use an absolute
# path ($HOME expands here; a quoted ~ does not). `install` copies whichever
# are set into the LaunchAgent.
# VAULT="$HOME/Documents/change-me Vault"
# REPO_ROOT="$HOME/repos"

# launchd label prefix shared by every system service (default
# local.system-tools). The dashboard's LaunchAgent is <prefix>.sd-dashboard.
# SYSTEM_TOOLS_LABEL_PREFIX=local.system-tools

# Failed-job Jev shadow on Now (sd:2095). Unset means on; 0, off, false, no or
# disabled switches it off. `jev off` switches every Jev stage off.
# JEV_JOB_TRIAGE=off
